# LF everywhere: in the repository and in the working tree, Windows included.
#
# `eol=lf` is the second half of that and it is deliberate. `text=auto` alone
# would check out CRLF on Windows, and this repository is developed on Windows
# and tested on Linux runners - a file that differs between the two is a diff
# that appears and disappears depending on who looked.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.py   text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Golden fixtures are compared byte for byte. Any translation here would make a
# contract test pass on one platform and fail on another.
tests/contract/golden/** -text
*.wav binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary

# Captured output, not authored text.
#
# Files under docs/evidence are the unedited stdout of a test or pilot run.
# Their value is that nobody touched them, so git's whitespace checks are
# turned off here rather than the bytes being tidied to satisfy them: a
# trailing space inside a pytest progress bar is part of what the run printed,
# and `git diff --check` flagging it would push somebody toward editing
# evidence.
#
# Appended, not substituted. An earlier version of this change replaced the
# file wholesale and silently dropped the LF normalization, the byte-for-byte
# guarantee on the golden fixtures above, and the binary declarations - none of
# which would have failed a test until a contract test disagreed with itself
# across two platforms.
docs/evidence/** -whitespace
